1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,990
Many believe that the story first began in America in 1877,

2
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:11,564
when two friends were arguing over whether a horse ever had all four feet

3
00:00:11,564 --> 00:00:14,530
or hooves off the ground when it galloped.

4
00:00:14,530 --> 00:00:20,144
To settle the bet, a photographer was asked to photograph a horse galloping

5
00:00:20,144 --> 00:00:24,815
and the bet was settled because you could see that all the hooves were

6
00:00:24,815 --> 00:00:26,940
off the ground in some of the photos.

7
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:32,734
What was even more interesting was that if the photos were shown in quick

8
00:00:32,734 --> 00:00:38,930
succession the horse looked like it was running-in other words 'moving pictures'.

9
00:00:38,930 --> 00:00:44,969
The person who became interested in taking the moving pictures to its next step

10
00:00:44,969 --> 00:00:48,600
was the famous American inventor Thomas Edison.

11
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:54,500
Actually, he didn't do the work himself but rather asked a young Scotsman in

12
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:56,346
his employ to design a system,

13
00:00:56,346 --> 00:00:57,570
which he did.

14
00:00:57,570 --> 00:01:03,340
Now this young fellow was clever because the first thing he did was study

15
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:10,633
other systems-primitive as they were-of moving pictures and then put all the existing technologies

16
00:01:10,633 --> 00:01:14,900
together to make the first entire motion picture system.

17
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:19,500
He designed a camera, a projection device and the film.

18
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:23,302
The system was first shown in New York in 1894

19
00:01:23,302 --> 00:01:26,400
and was really very popular.

20
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:31,410
Apparently people lined up around the block to see the wonderful new invention.

21
00:01:31,410 --> 00:01:35,640
There were, however, a couple of problems with the system.

22
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:41,966
The camera weighed over 200 kilograms and only one person at a time could

23
00:01:41,966 --> 00:01:42,850
see the film.

24
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:48,550
Well now, news of the new system in America travelled fast

25
00:01:48,550 --> 00:01:53,628
and a number of rival European system started to appear once people had heard

26
00:01:53,628 --> 00:01:54,270
about it.

27
00:01:54,270 --> 00:02:00,388
The single problem with all the systems was they couldn't really project the film

28
00:02:00,388 --> 00:02:02,373
onto a screen-you know,

29
00:02:02,373 --> 00:02:04,680
so more than one person could see it.

30
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:11,732
Then in 1895 three systems were all developed, more or less at the same time

31
00:02:11,732 --> 00:02:13,500
and independently of each other.

32
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:18,598
I guess the most famous of these was by the Lumiere Brothers from France,

33
00:02:18,598 --> 00:02:23,916
and they called their system the cinematograph which of course is where the word

34
00:02:23,916 --> 00:02:25,120
cinema comes from.

35
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:29,789
There were also two brothers in Germany who developed a successful system

36
00:02:29,789 --> 00:02:31,580
and they called it a bioskop.

37
00:02:31,580 --> 00:02:37,919
Well now, once the problem of projection had been solved, the next challenge for

38
00:02:37,919 --> 00:02:41,370
the inventors was to make the films longer and more interesting.

39
00:02:41,370 --> 00:02:46,143
A continuing problem at the time was that the films had a tendency to

40
00:02:46,143 --> 00:02:52,480
break when they were being played-a problem which was caused by the tension between the two wheels

41
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:54,886
or 'reels' as they are called,

42
00:02:54,886 --> 00:02:56,230
which hold the film.

43
00:02:56,230 --> 00:03:00,870
Now this problem was solved by two American brothers.

44
00:03:00,870 --> 00:03:03,978
They developed the 'Lantham Loop',

45
00:03:03,978 --> 00:03:08,208
which was the simple addition of a third reel between the two main reels,

46
00:03:08,208 --> 00:03:12,739
and this took all the tension away with the result that the film stopped

47
00:03:12,739 --> 00:03:13,300
snapping.

48
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:18,272
So now there was a real possibility of having films of more than two

49
00:03:18,272 --> 00:03:19,034
or three minutes,

50
00:03:19,034 --> 00:03:23,564
and this led to the making of The Great Train Robbery - the very

51
00:03:23,564 --> 00:03:25,890
first movie made.

52
00:03:25,890 --> 00:03:31,022
It only lasted 11 minutes but was an absolute sensation,

53
00:03:31,022 --> 00:03:35,954
and there were cases of people watching the movie and actually fainting when the

54
00:03:35,954 --> 00:03:38,260
character fired a gun at the camera!

55
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:42,370
Almost overnight movies became a craze,

56
00:03:42,370 --> 00:03:48,486
and by 1905 people in America were lining up to see movies in 'store

57
00:03:48,486 --> 00:03:50,270
theatres', as they were called then.

58
00:03:50,270 --> 00:03:56,405
I guess the next big step in terms of development of technology was to

59
00:03:56,405 --> 00:03:59,012
have people actually talking on the film,

60
00:03:59,012 --> 00:04:05,267
and the first step towards this was in 1926 when sound effects were first

61
00:04:05,267 --> 00:04:06,370
used on a film.

62
00:04:06,370 --> 00:04:12,154
It wasn't until the following year however that the first 'talkie', as they were

63
00:04:12,154 --> 00:04:13,560
called then, was made.

64
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:18,129
This film featured actors speaking only during parts of the film

65
00:04:18,129 --> 00:04:20,253
and was called The Jazz Singer,

66
00:04:20,253 --> 00:04:26,244
and it wasn't until 1928 that the first all-talking film was produced,

67
00:04:26,244 --> 00:04:29,430
and this was called The Lights of New York.

68
00:04:29,430 --> 00:04:34,182
Unfortunately, the sound on this early film was not very good

69
00:04:34,182 --> 00:04:40,298
and I believe they put subtitles on the film-that is, they printed the dialogue

70
00:04:40,298 --> 00:04:44,770
along the bottom of the film to compensate for this poor sound quality.

71
00:04:44,770 --> 00:04:51,450
Now, with the addition of sound, moving pictures became far more difficult to make...
